You didn't want to talk. You unwillingly nodded in response with a dissatisfied grimace on your face.
"Had a rough morning?" the bearded man kept asking.
"Hell yeah," you snapped.
"Splendid!" cried the man, "you are a typical werewolf."
"What?" you didn't understand. 
"You see, I didn't work as a truck driver my whole life. Actually, I'm a philosopher. But living on a teacher's salary...," he sighed, "anyway, so seeing you, I somehow remembered a mythical creature. A werewolf."
"Nobody bit me," you said, though you weren’t sure why.
"That is not necessary to become one!" said the man, happy to find you finally opening up to conversation.
You were all ears.
"It is enough just to put a wolf pelt on, pronounce a spell, and the job is done."
A waitress brought some tea, but you were deep in thought. You recollected that a few days ago you visited the country home of an acquaintance of yours. And you actually tried a wolf pelt on there...
"And the most interesting part," the philosopher continued, "the best way to kill such a werewolf is to drive a hawthorn stake into him. I see you don't want your tea. I'll drink it while it's warm." After those words the man took the glass and took a large gulp before you could stop him.
"Ah-h, wonderful. You should have drunk the tea, it's very good." You turned purple with rage, stood up and nearly ran out of the bar, trying not to hear what your new acquaintance was saying behind your back. You decided to take rest in the neighboring grove and in the evening go visit the scoundrel who had put a spell on you.  You hid in the shrubs and fell asleep, lulled by the peaceful buzzing of bees. When your dreams released you, it was already dark. You were starving. You left your shelter and noticed the philosopher leave the bar. Anger seized you like a whirlpool. 